  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x201c;SANEAMIENTO B&#xc1;SICO Y PRODUCCI&#xd3;N ORG&#xc1;NICA DE ALIMENTOS EN LA MANCOMUNIDAD DE AZARI&#x201d;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Azari, is a semi&#x2013;urban settlement in Sucre and is mainly inhabited by families who have migrated there from the surrounding province of Chuquisaca. People in these spontaneous settlements do not have titles that guarantee them the ownership of their plots, and their homes have no basic services such as electricity, potable water and sewer systems, increasing the risk of diarrhoea and other diseases. 
 
Since the households in these settlements mainly come from rural areas, and since their houses occupy a small portion of their plots, urban agriculture is an alternative that will help improve nutrition levels; particularly of children. Likewise, their homes have tin roofs that facilitate the harvesting of water. 

As part of the Programme seeking to address issues of water contamination and scarcity in the Altoplano, ACLO Chuquisaca will target impoverished households of indigenous origin settled in neighbourhoods that do not have basic services or access to water, to enable them to cover their daily and long-term water needs, as well as produce vegetables that will enrich the family diet. 

ACLO is experienced in advocacy and leadership capacity strengthening, and therefore they will also work with grassroots organizations and community leaders to foster the involvement of the municipal government in the realisation of development projects and the application of housing standards within the scope of national development policies.</narrative>
  </description>
